<div class="card mt-3">
  <div class="card-body">
    <h4 class="card-title">Known Collexia System Behaviors</h4>
    <p class="text-muted small mb-3">
      Confirmed facts about how Collexia's own system behaves, gathered from their UAT Test Pack and
      direct testing -- kept here so this knowledge lives in DesertLedger itself, not just in email/chat
      threads with Collexia's team.
    </p>
    <table class="table table-sm table-borderless mb-0">
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td style="width: 260px;" class="text-nowrap"><strong>Weekend/holiday collections</strong></td>
          <td>
            If a mandate's Next Date For Collection (or a rescheduled installment date) falls on a
            weekend or public holiday, Collexia automatically moves collection to the <strong>next
            business day</strong> -- it never collects early, and DesertLedger doesn't need to
            pre-adjust the date. Also shown as inline help on the Start Date / Reschedule fields.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="text-nowrap"><strong>Tracking Days</strong></td>
          <td>
            The number of days (1-14) Collexia keeps retrying a collection attempt after the scheduled
            date before giving up and marking it failed. Set per debit order at registration
            (<code>no_of_days_tracking</code>) -- there is no way to change it after placement without
            rescheduling the installment.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="text-nowrap"><strong>Past collection/reschedule dates</strong></td>
          <td>
            Collexia rejects a mandate load or installment update whose date isn't in the future
            ("10567 Registration declined" / "10568 Update Declined -- Collection Date must be in the
            future"). DesertLedger now blocks this locally too (date pickers refuse a past date, and the
            server re-checks it), so this should never reach Collexia at all.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="text-nowrap"><strong>Amount limits (UAT-only)</strong></td>
          <td>
            Both "10569 Mandate amount limit exceeded" (whole mandate) and "10570 Installment amount
            limit exceeded" (a single installment) are confirmed by Collexia to be sandbox/UAT-only
            restrictions that will not exist in production. Only the mandate-level one has a local guard
            today (Max Single Mandate Amount, above) -- remember to blank it before go-live.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="text-nowrap"><strong>CDV account validation</strong></td>
          <td>
            Collexia validates the debtor's account number and account type against the receiving
            bank's own records (CDV) before accepting a mandate -- "5645" rejects a bad account number,
            "5646" a mismatched account type, and "5647" a general CDV error (account number, account
            type, or branch). These come back as plain rejection text, which DesertLedger already
            surfaces as-is.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="text-nowrap"><strong>UAT test accounts</strong></td>
          <td>
            Collexia's UAT Test Pack supplies dummy account numbers for forcing specific outcomes while
            testing (Frozen, Non Existing, Deceased/Dormant, Suspended, Closed, Active, Inactive) -- see
            the Test Pack's "UAT Test Accounts" sheet rather than using a real client account for
            negative-path testing.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>
